WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.946
I'm Lucas Dul, and I've been repairing

00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:13.410
and restoring typewriters
for nearly a decade.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.217
And I'm going to show you how to restore

00:00:15.217 --> 00:00:18.060
a 90-year-old Royal typewriter.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.160
This particular machine was purchased new

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.190
in India by a family member of my client.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.230
To him, it is a family heirloom.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.538
The serial number places it

00:00:26.538 --> 00:00:28.980
in the second half of 1930.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.146
In its current condition,

00:00:30.146 --> 00:00:32.196
it is not a functioning machine.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.270
The first pieces to come
off of the typewriter

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.580
are the platen knobs
as well as the platen,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.223
which is going to be replaced.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.920
And then I'll remove the body shells.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.870
I'll also remove the paper table,

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.587
the bail roller assembly,

00:00:47.587 --> 00:00:50.043
the paper tray, and the feed rollers.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.610
Usually, carriage disassembly stops there,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.968
but on cases of really rough machines,

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:58.151
such as this one, the internal type bars

00:00:58.151 --> 00:01:00.330
and the actuator rods are all removed

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.455
from the machine and set
aside for individual cleaning.

00:01:06.574 --> 00:01:09.612
The first step is to pass
with an air compressor

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:12.523
to dislodge large sections
of dirt and debris.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.525
Here, I'm using a wire
brush attachment on a Dremel

00:01:19.525 --> 00:01:21.993
to get into hard-to-reach places.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.280
After the bulk of the rust is removed,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.255
everything gets a pass
with mineral spirits

00:01:30.255 --> 00:01:32.133
with a soft-bristle brush.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.086
After the cleaning is finished,

00:01:37.086 --> 00:01:38.861
all of the bare metal surfaces get wiped

00:01:38.861 --> 00:01:41.043
with a rag to remove any residue.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.591
The keyboard on this machine

00:01:46.591 --> 00:01:48.682
was also intensely discolored.

00:01:49.616 --> 00:01:53.010
Each key ring is carefully
removed from the key top.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.038
I'm using a special key-ring-remover plyer

00:01:55.038 --> 00:01:57.270
from the early 1900s.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.550
Anytime that you're working
with a glass keyboard,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.680
you run the risk of breaking keys.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.770
Due to the age of the tool
and the hardened rubber,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.347
about half of these glass circles

00:02:06.347 --> 00:02:08.590
in the keyboard ended up shattering.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.450
Once all of the keys are disassembled,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.600
the paper legends are removed.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.810
The individual glass
circles also get cleaned.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.912
Here I'm using a razor blade to scrape off

00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:25.630
all of the accumulated corrosion

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.750
that is stuck to the surface of the glass.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.150
Then they're each
individually hand-polished

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.548
with a fine-grade polishing compound.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.430
If new glass rings need to be fabricated,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.435
they're cut by hand from around

00:02:40.435 --> 00:02:42.873
a 16th-inch-thick piece of glass.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.750
This is a common glass standard thickness

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.092
for picture frames.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.190
At this point, any
springs that are missing

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.320
on the typewriter get replaced.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.030
Over time, they can sometimes stretch

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.310
or can become damaged if a machine falls.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.680
Missing or damaged
pieces are also replaced.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.338
I also create new feed rollers

00:03:06.338 --> 00:03:07.713
using old rubber hosing

00:03:07.713 --> 00:03:09.753
or sometimes layers of heat shrink.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.350
Now I install the paper table.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.058
This is the piece of metal that holds

00:03:18.058 --> 00:03:19.620
both the feed rollers in place

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.650
and guides the paper around the platen.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.732
The most difficult part about assembling

00:03:24.732 --> 00:03:26.970
Royal typewriters are the ball bearings.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.400
Here I'm using a drinking
straw to insert them

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.900
into the proper placement
along the carriage rail.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.560
Another tricky part is the ribbon vibrator

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.090
or the ribbon lifting fork.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.690
Here you can see me attaching it

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.273
with a small cotter pin.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.630
The key legends also need
to be carefully placed

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.880
so that they're aligned vertically.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.970
Each key ring needs to be seated properly

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.310
with the key-ring-installation tool,

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.560
and then once each key top on the machine

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.420
is completely set, the bottom plate

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.870
of the key-ring-installation
tool gets rotated

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.522
to a flat portion of the plate,

00:04:08.522 --> 00:04:10.730
which is used to seat down all of the tabs

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.013
on the bottom of the key ring

00:04:12.013 --> 00:04:14.613
firmly against the bottom of the key cup.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.770
The second-to-last step

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.263
is to reassemble everything completely.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.200
All typewriters are individually adjusted

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.320
so that the parts work well together.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.385
So when you take certain
parts from one machine

00:04:33.385 --> 00:04:35.151
to fill into another machine

00:04:35.151 --> 00:04:36.570
that is missing those parts,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.070
you will need to adjust those parts

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.894
to work with each other.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.480
Once everything is carefully together,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.100
I install the brand-new rubber

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.492
and the feet as well as a new ribbon.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.538
Once the ribbon is in the machine,

00:04:59.538 --> 00:05:01.305
the type testing begins.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.795
The primary things that I'm looking for

00:05:04.795 --> 00:05:06.303
are evenness of the letters,

00:05:06.303 --> 00:05:08.561
alignment between the
capital and lowercase,

00:05:08.561 --> 00:05:12.150
margin consistency, and line scale height.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.620
The escapement must also be adjusted

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.750
so that it is moving the proper distance

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.203
both up and down to prevent
skipping or missed spaces.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.290
Then I move on to adjusting
the height of the ribbon

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.173
to ensure that the entire
letter is being printed.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.160
The case is usually a secondary thing

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.230
to the typewriter that
I generally don't spend

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.430
a lot of time with,

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.280
but this particular one
needed a little bit of care.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.080
Unfortunately, the base of the case,

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.271
where it mounts to the machine,

00:05:47.271 --> 00:05:49.290
had two strips of wood in place

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.750
of the four feet that
are supposed to be there

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.450
and no way to actually
mount the machine to it.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.696
So here I'm removing these word rails

00:05:56.696 --> 00:05:59.400
and then drilling and
counter-sinking holes

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.677
for the screws and the feet

00:06:00.677 --> 00:06:02.763
that will mount the machine to the base.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.809
The final step is to
cut a strip of leather

00:06:08.809 --> 00:06:12.033
and to punch holes to stitch
a new handle onto the case.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.840
This is probably one of the worst Royals

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.430
that I have seen, cosmetically.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.637
That being said,
theoretically, there is no

00:06:28.637 --> 00:06:31.500
amount of damage that is
physically irreparable.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.332
However, there does come
a point where the cost

00:06:33.332 --> 00:06:36.090
of repair far exceeds
the value of the machine,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.283
both monetarily and sentimentally.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.470
And in essence, that is how I
restore antique typewriters.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.470
Any machine from the
mid-1800s to as late as 2020

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.420
follows pretty much the
same exact procedures.

